-- title:
--   The rational endomorphism ring of an elliptic curve is a domain
-- statement:
--   Let $k$ be an algebraically closed field and let $X$ be a Weierstrass curve over $k$ which is elliptic (`X.IsElliptic`, i.e. its discriminant is a unit). Consider the group $(X_{k})(k)$ of affine points of the base change of $X$ along $k \to k$, written `(X.baseChange k).toAffine.Point`, and inside its additive endomorphism monoid `AddMonoid.End` the subset [`WeierstrassCurve.rationalHomSet k X X`](def/WeierstrassCurve_RationalEnd.html#L28) consisting of those additive endomorphisms $\alpha$ that are either zero or satisfy the predicate `IsRationallyRepresented k X X`, which records that $\alpha$ is given by a rational formula in the coordinates. The ring [`WeierstrassCurve.rationalEndSubring k X`](def/WeierstrassCurve_RationalEnd.html#L32) is by definition the subring of `AddMonoid.End (X.baseChange k).toAffine.Point` generated by this subset. The assertion is that this subring is a domain: it is nontrivial and has no zero divisors (an `IsDomain` instance; commutativity is not asserted, the multiplication being composition of endomorphisms).
--
--   This is the standard statement that the endomorphism ring of an elliptic curve over an algebraically closed field has no zero divisors, here for the subring generated by the rationally represented additive endomorphisms. It is used in the construction of the endomorphism algebra as a definite quaternion algebra, feeding [`WeierstrassCurve.exists_isDefiniteRamifiedExactlyAt_isMaximalOrder_range_eq_rationalEndSubring`](thm.html#WeierstrassCurve.exists_isDefiniteRamifiedExactlyAt_isMaximalOrder_range_eq_rationalEndSubring).
